      "title": "Assistant Professor - Environmental Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Environmental Engineering - Houston, Texas, United States Assistant Professor - Environmental Engineering - Houston, Texas, United States Kiosk mode Assistant Professor - Environmental Engineering 497034 Full-Time Civil Engineering Position Description Department : Civil and Environmental Engineering Salary : Commensurate with experience Description : The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at the University of Houston seeks an exceptional scholar to expand its research and educational programs in environmental engineering at the rank of Assistant Professor. Areas of particular research interest include, but are not limited to, air quality engineering; environmental sensing and monitoring; indoor air quality and the built environment; emerging environmental pollutants; resource recovery and reuse; and environmental and community health. Candidates whose research integrates advanced sensing technologies, artificial intelligence, data analytics, or data-driven modeling with experimental or field-based environmental engineering are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will have opportunities to develop collaborations with researchers across the University in environmental engineering, atmospheric sciences, public health, data science, energy, materials, and related areas and to utilize shared Departmental, College, and University research facilities. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate education in environmental engineering, including instruction in areas that complement the Department's existing strengths in water and advanced treatment systems and that engage emerging research directions. This position advances the University of Houston Research Priorities in Energy, Security and Sustainability through research addressing environmental sustainability, water and resource security, pollution prevention, and resource recovery, and in Health and Well-being through research linking environmental quality, the built environment, and community health. Research incorporating advanced sensing, artificial intelligence, and environmental data science also aligns with the Digital Futures and the Human Experience priority. Qualifications Applicants must have an earned Ph.D. in Civil Engineering, Environmental Engineering, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Candidates are expected to develop and maintain an internationally recognized and externally funded research program; publish high-impact scholarship; mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers; contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching; and participate in professional and University service. Candidates whose scholarship creates collaborations across traditional disciplinary boundaries and complements existing research strengths within the Civil and Environmental Engineering Department, the Cullen College of Engineering, and the broader University of Houston research enterprise are particularly encouraged to apply. University, College, and Department The University of Houston is a Carnegie-designated Tier One public research university recognized with a Phi Beta Kappa chapter for excellence in undergraduate education. UH serves the globally competitive Houston and Gulf Coast region by providing world-class faculty, experiential learning and strategic industry partnerships. Located in the nation's fourth-largest city and one of the most vibrant regions in the country, UH enrolls nearly 49,000 students. The Cullen College of Engineering is a major center for engineering research and education, with more than $58 million in annual research expenditures, more than 200 tenured and tenure-track faculty, and extensive research infrastructure supporting interdisciplinary scholarship. Cullen is ranked among the Top 50 public graduate engineering schools in the United States. Located within one of the nation’s largest engineering and technology economies, the College has strong connections with Houston’s energy, aerospace, health care, and technology sectors, creating exceptional opportunities for industry partnership, technology translation, and research impact. The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering conducts interdisciplinary research aimed at advancing the sustainability and resilience of natural and built environments. Its approximately 20 tenure-track faculty members work across environmental engineering, hydrosystems, geosensing, geotechnical and structural engineering, and mechanics, using advanced sensing, computational modeling, and other emerging technologies. Houston and the Gulf Coast provide a distinctive living laboratory for research on water systems, flooding and coastal hazards, infrastructure, environmental quality, and urban resilience, with substantial opportunities for collaboration with public agencies, industry, and other research institutions. How to Apply Applications should include: • a cover letter identifying the position for which the candidate wishes to be considered (Hydrology/Water Resources or Environmental Engineering or both); • a curriculum vitae; • a research statement describing past accomplishments and future research plans, not exceeding two pages; • a teaching statement describing teaching philosophy, expertise, and interests, not exceeding two pages; and • names and complete contact information for four professional references. Applications should be submitted through the University of Houston Careers website at https://www.uh.edu/human-resources/careers/ . The anticipated start date is Fall 2027. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Employees are expected to have a home address in the state of Texas for the duration of their appointments. Official transcripts are required for a faculty appointment and will be requested upon selection of the final candidates. All positions at the University of Houston-System are security sensitive and will require a criminal history check. Individuals conducting research in critical infrastructure areas (i.e., communication infrastructure systems, cybersecurity systems, electric grid, hazardous waste treatment systems, and/or water treatment facilities) are subject to regular review to ensure the security and integrity of the research is maintained. The University of Houston System and its universities are Equal Opportunity Institutions. Everyone is encouraged to apply. Apply now First Name (required) ca361042 Last Name (required) d475ddbe Email (required) 26b31519 Cell number 0f082fc4 244 results found No results found Afghanistan (+93) Åland Islands (+358) Albania (+355) Algeria (+213) American Samoa (+1) Andorra (+376) Angola (+244) Anguilla (+1) Antigua & Barbuda (+1) Argentina (+54) Armenia (+374) Aruba (+297) Ascension Island (+247) Australia (+61) Austria (+43) Azerbaijan (+994) Bahamas (+1) Bahrain (+973) Bangladesh (+880) Barbados (+1) Belarus (+375) Belgium (+32) Belize (+501) Benin (+229) Bermuda (+1) Bhutan (+975) Bolivia (+591) Bosnia & Herzegovina (+387) Botswana (+267) Brazil (+55) British Indian Ocean Territory (+246) British Virgin Islands (+1) Brunei (+673) Bulgaria (+359) Burkina Faso (+226) Burundi (+257) Cambodia (+855) Cameroon (+237) Canada (+1) Cape Verde (+238) Caribbean Netherlands (+599) Cayman Islands (+1) Central African Republic (+236) Chad (+235) Chile (+56) China (+86) Christmas Island (+61) Cocos (Keeling) Islands (+61) Colombia (+57) Comoros (+269) Congo - Brazzaville (+242) Congo - Kinshasa (+243) Cook Islands (+682) Costa Rica (+506) Côte d’Ivoire (+225) Croatia (+385) Cuba (+53) Curaçao (+599) Cyprus (+357) Czechia (+420) Denmark (+45) Djibouti (+253) Dominica (+1) Dominican Republic (+1) Ecuador (+593) Egypt (+20) El Salvador (+503) Equatorial Guinea (+240) Eritrea (+291) Estonia (+372) Eswatini (+268) Ethiopia (+251) Falkland Islands (Islas Malvinas) (+500) Faroe Islands (+298) Fiji (+679) Finland (+358) France (+33) Frenc